---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const pageTitle = 'About';
const description =
  'PlainKit is a static site with no server, no tracking and no accounts. Here is exactly how that works, and how to verify it.';
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={description}>
  <article class="tool">
    <header class="tool__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="tool__title">How this works</h1>
      <p class="tool__lead">
        PlainKit is a set of everyday tools that do their work on your device. Here is what that
        means, and how you can check it yourself.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="tool__notes">
      <h2 class="tool__notes-title">There is no server</h2>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        This is a static site. It is a folder of HTML, CSS and JavaScript files served straight to
        your browser. There is no application server, no database and no user accounts, so there is
        nowhere for your input to be stored even if we wanted to.
      </p>

      <h2 class="tool__notes-title">The browser blocks it, not us</h2>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        Every page sends a Content-Security-Policy rule called <code>connect-src 'none'</code>. That
        tells the browser to refuse any network connection the page tries to make. So "nothing
        leaves your device" is not a promise you have to take on faith — it is enforced by the
        browser.
      </p>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        To verify it, open any tool and run this in your browser console while you use it:
      </p>
      <code class="code-block">
        performance.getEntriesByType('resource').filter((r) =&gt;
        !r.name.startsWith(location.origin))
      </code>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">The result is an empty list. There is nothing to call out to.</p>

      <h2 class="tool__notes-title">No ads, no tracking, no accounts</h2>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        There are no advertising slots, no analytics, no cookies, no session recording and no
        third-party scripts of any kind. Every tool is open the moment the page loads, because there
        is nothing to sign up for.
      </p>

      <h2 class="tool__notes-title">Host it yourself</h2>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        PlainKit is open source under Apache-2.0. The build produces a plain folder of static files,
        so you can serve it from anywhere. If you prefer a container, the repository ships a
        Dockerfile and a compose file:
      </p>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        <code>docker compose up</code>
      </p>
      <p class="tool__notes-body">
        The site then runs at <code>http://localhost:8080</code>. Without Docker, run
        <code>npm install</code> and <code>npm run build</code>, then serve the <code>dist</code>{' '}
        folder.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
